summary:"The Languedoc pyramid and its new standalone crus, Limoux's sparkling rules, Roussillon's villages, Provence's terroirs and Bandol.",
body:`
<h3>The Languedoc pyramid</h3>
<p>Coteaux du Languedoc became <b>AOC Languedoc</b> in 2007. Since 2011 the region has used a three-step pyramid: <b>Languedoc</b> (regional) → <b>Grands Vins du Languedoc</b> (named appellations, about 70% of production) → <b>Crus du Languedoc</b> (the top). The crus have been carved out one by one as standalone AOCs:</p>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Cru</th><th>Standalone from</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Picpoul de Pinet</td><td>2013</td><td>Saline Picpoul whites near the Thau lagoon</td></tr>
<tr><td>Terrasses du Larzac</td><td>2014</td><td>Red only, at least three varieties; cool nights from the Larzac plateau</td></tr>
<tr><td>La Clape</td><td>2015</td><td>Limestone massif by the sea; Bourboulenc whites</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pic Saint-Loup</td><td>2017</td><td>Red and rosé; Syrah-led; about 1,470 ha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Boutenac</td><td>March 2024</td><td>Red only; now separate from Corbières</td></tr>
<tr><td>La Livinière</td><td>March 2024</td><td>Red only; now separate from Minervois</td></tr></table>
<p>Older materials that print "Corbières-Boutenac" and "Minervois-La Livinière" are out of date. Other names: <b>Faugères</b> (schist), <b>Saint-Chinian</b> (schist in the north with the Berlou and Roquebrun designations, clay-limestone in the south), Minervois, Corbières, Fitou, Malepère, Cabardès. Grapes: Grenache, Syrah, Mourvèdre, Carignan (old vines prized), Cinsault; whites from Grenache Blanc, Roussanne, Marsanne, Vermentino, Picpoul, Bourboulenc. Producers: Mas Jullien, Domaine de l'Hortus, Mas de Daumas Gassac (IGP pioneer), Clos Marie, Domaine Gauby (Roussillon), Château de Pech-Redon.</p>
<h3>Limoux: France's other sparkling heartland</h3>
<ul><li><b>Blanquette de Limoux:</b> at least 90% Mauzac, traditional method, 9 months on lees.</li>
<li><b>Blanquette Méthode Ancestrale:</b> 100% Mauzac, one fermentation finishing in bottle, about 6-7% alcohol, sweet.</li>
<li><b>Crémant de Limoux:</b> Chardonnay and Chenin Blanc together 60-90% (Chenin at least 10%), Mauzac and Pinot Noir at most 40% (Mauzac at most 20%).</li>
<li><b>Limoux</b> still whites (barrel-fermented Chardonnay) and reds (at least 50% Merlot).</li></ul>
<h3>Roussillon</h3>
<p><b>Côtes du Roussillon Villages</b> is red only, from about 50 communes along the Agly valley, with four named villages tied to soil: <b>Caramany</b> (gneiss), <b>Latour-de-France</b> (schist), <b>Lesquerde</b> (granite) and <b>Tautavel</b> (clay-limestone), plus Les Aspres in the south. Blends need at least two varieties and no single grape above 80%. <b>Collioure</b> makes dry reds, whites and rosés on the same terraced schist as <b>Banyuls</b> (VDN; Banyuls Grand Cru needs at least 75% Grenache Noir). <b>Maury</b> now also makes a dry red (Maury Sec).</p>
<h3>Provence</h3>
<p>Provence is <b>89% rosé</b>, 6% white, 5% red. Côtes de Provence makes about 73% of the rosé, Coteaux d'Aix-en-Provence 16%, Coteaux Varois 11%. The US (37%) and UK (18%) are its biggest export markets. Côtes de Provence has five geographic designations: <b>Sainte-Victoire</b> (renamed <b>Cru Sainte-Victoire</b> in May 2026), <b>Fréjus</b>, <b>La Londe</b> (the only one also allowed to make white), <b>Pierrefeu</b> and <b>Notre-Dame des Anges</b> (from 2019). Designation rosés cannot use charcoal to lighten color, and yields are lower (50 hl/ha for most). Rosé is made by direct pressing or short maceration; color is measured and marketed almost like a brand.</p>
<p><b>Bandol</b> (AOC 1941, 8 communes, terraced restanques) is 75% rosé, 21% red, 4% white. Reds need <b>at least 50% Mourvèdre and 18 months in cask</b>, vines over 7 years old, 40 hl/ha, and hand harvesting. In August 2026 the rules added climate-adaptation varieties for whites, including Assyrtiko. Domaine Tempier, Château Pradeaux, Domaine de Terrebrune, Château de Pibarnon. Small historic AOCs: <b>Cassis</b> (whites), <b>Bellet</b> (above Nice; Braquet, Folle Noire, Rolle), <b>Palette</b> (Château Simone), <b>Les Baux-de-Provence</b> (organic pioneers like Domaine de Trévallon, often sold as IGP).</p>`,
floor:`<p>Provence rosé is a big seller; know that pale doesn't mean better, and point guests to Bandol rosé for something with structure and age-worthiness. Languedoc crus are the list's best value reds.</p>`,
